rationale:
The working-tree file /Users/justinlebar/code/llvm2/llvm/lib/Target/NVPTX/NVPTXISelLowering.cpp ALREADY contains a correct, minimal fix for this exact bug (uncommitted on branch fix9-wip; HEAD still has the buggy code). The fix inserted right after the `if (!MatchedUGT && !MatchedULT) return SDValue();` check is:

  // The guard compares the full-width ShiftAmt, but the clamp uses the (possibly
  // narrower, e.g. i32) shift-amount operand of the shift. If ShiftAmt is wider,
  // the clamp drops its high bits; the rewrite is only sound when those dropped
  // bits are known zero, so that the narrowed amount and the guard agree on
  // whether the shift amount is < BitWidth.
  SDValue ClampAmt = ShiftOp.getOperand(1);
  unsigned ClampAmtBits = ClampAmt.getValueSizeInBits();
  if (ShiftAmt.getValueSizeInBits() > ClampAmtBits &&
      DCI.DAG.computeKnownBits(ShiftAmt).countMaxActiveBits() > ClampAmtBits)
    return SDValue();

and the final rewrite now passes `ClampAmt` instead of `ShiftOp.getOperand(1)`.

I verified this fix is sound: ShiftAmt is the value the SetCC compared (wide, e.g. i64); ClampAmt is the actual shift operand (e.g. i32 trunc of ShiftAmt). When ShiftAmt is wider AND its computeKnownBits max-active-bits exceed ClampAmtBits (the repro case: %s is a param, 64 active bits > 32), the truncation can lose value-distinguishing high bits, so the combine bails and the original full-width guard is preserved -> correct result 0. When ShiftAmt's value provably fits in ClampAmtBits (countMaxActiveBits <= ClampAmtBits, e.g. %s = zext i32), trunc is lossless so `ShiftAmt > BW-1` <=> `ClampAmt > BW-1` and the clamp is sound; this beneficial case is still taken. When there is no truncation (widths equal, the m_TruncOrSelf self-branch — the common case) the first condition is false and the combine fires unchanged.

I am returning an empty `files` list because the fix is already present verbatim in the current working-tree file; supplying old_string from the buggy HEAD text would fail to match the working tree (the buggy `ShiftOp.getOperand(1)` and the missing guard no longer exist there). The only remaining action is to rebuild llc (the build/ binary is stale and still reflects HEAD); after `ninja llc` the repro returns 0 for both functions, matching x86_64.

For reference, the equivalent diff against the buggy committed HEAD (the real upstream patch) replaces:
  return DCI.DAG.getNode(ClampOpc, SDLoc(N), ShiftOp.getValueType(),
                         ShiftOp.getOperand(0), ShiftOp.getOperand(1));
with the guard block above plus passing ClampAmt — but that edit must not be applied because it is already applied.

